WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.440
i'm sorry, i'm sorry

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.520
i know many people hate it

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.700
but the jump cut, is here to stay

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.500
and this is why

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.780
so i'm personally a bit happy about doing this video

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.280
because it gives me a a textual excuse

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.940
to go back to jump cuts for a video

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.000
this video is part of a follow up to two other videos

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.800
the first being an outline

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.800
of the basic language of film editing

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.200
basic cuts where jump cuts were mentioned

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.040
and demonstrated, and many people in the comments

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.220
suggested a deeper dive into the specific uses

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.520
and aesthetic of the jump cut.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.220
Then as well, this is a follow up to last week's video

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.680
on the dialect of vlogging

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.960
and specifically points made in that video

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.200
about how the use of jump cuts has spread to youtube

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.160
as a whole

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.160
has become ingrained in the basic visual language

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.180
of the medium

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.080
in that video i asked people what they think

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.160
youtube will look like in ten years

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.420
and people, including crown prince of logarithm, hank

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.180
green, generally agreed that the jump cut

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.100
would remain eternal, and i agree

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.860
now i have made use of the jump cut before

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.080
and not just in the way that i will

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.600
occasionally let one slide because it's going to be too

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.240
much of a hassle to cut around

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.400
when i first moved to the current channel

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.880
following the shutdown of blip.tv

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.980
i chose to adopt a fairly conventional youtube tone

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.860
both to feel it out for myself

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.600
as i've always been of a mind to tinker with

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.700
how i structure the channel and to

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.040
blend in with the locals as it were

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.020
so i have some experience with jump cuts as both

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.140
an aesthetic and as a workflow

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.360
and that's how we're going to break this up

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.960
two broad categories, speaking to why the jump cut

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:42.540
is here to stay

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.080
the first being the semantic and aesthetic reasons

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.220
the second being the pragmatic

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.100
and i'm going to start this off by outright praising

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.780
what i see is the unique semantic strength

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.880
of the jump cut and that is its high degree

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.460
of semiotic flexibility

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.420
i'm actually a big fan of the teleporting jump cut

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.500
and for a while, i explicitly used

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.200
a right-to-left teleport to indicate parenthetic statements

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.840
this was something i more or less lifted

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.760
directly from say, frank, hank green and nice peter

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.880
because it is a very effective form of visual punctuation

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.780
with different areas of the set and the frame used

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.900
to indicate different types of statements

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.160
it also really helps to break up long complex ideas

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.700
into smaller more manageable bites

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.880
this can serve to properly differentiate a joke

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.880
from a serious statement by moving either, very far from

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.600
or very close to the camera or, going back

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.760
to my own example, it can create an aside

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.100
by literally moving aside

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.120
this all has a direct narrative value in helping

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.620
better communicate meaning

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.620
which is great when you're trying to get across

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.860
a complex idea or unambiguously indicate

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.540
that something is a joke

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.140
now, many of these techniques like

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.900
dramatically changing framing for emphasis can be

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.900
replicated with conventional cutting

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.880
this gag with brock mansley where i keep zooming

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.480
closer and closer for comedic effect, has the same

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.740
root idea which is replicating a sense of unease

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.700
and invasion of personal space that would come along

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.100
with physically moving myself closer to the camera

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.080
however there's a specific device

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.180
that can only be achieved by using jump cuts

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.980
and that is the use of the set, as a stage

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.680
when a performer opts to set their camera and

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.620
eave the framing unchanged, the resulting video

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.800
takes on more of the characteristics of theatre

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.660
where specific locations within the stage

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.920
take on meanings that aren't properly

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.480
replicated purely by position within the frame

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.360
while a close-up works to create a psychological sense

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.680
of closeness that effect is amplified

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.100
if it's cast against a fixed canvas

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.540
that more directly communicates the speaker

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.540
moving physically closer to the camera

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.780
and by proxy, the viewer

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.340
all of the meanings of depths are made more potent

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:59.580
by giving more meaning to near and far

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:03.300
likewise moving to the left to indicate an aside

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.440
is more potent the more meaning left has

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.800
if left is a physical characteristic of the space

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.280
rather than simply an implication created

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.660
by the movement of the camera

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:20.840
to put another way stage left is more meaningful

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.400
than frame left

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.560
what this translates into is the jump-cut style

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.560
allows for a degree of intimacy that other editing styles

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.980
can't quite replicate by virtue of how it creates a stage

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.700
rather than just a frame, this ability to set a stage

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.020
and in that stage create a replica

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.760
of the intimacy of theater

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.820
is i think a unique strength of the style

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:47.880
i wanted to emphasize this first

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.060
because i do think it's important to defend

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:51.880
the aesthetic value of the jump-cut

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.040
rather than just saying, well it solves a bunch

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.699
of pragmatic problems

00:04:56.699 --> 00:04:58.199
speaking of that

00:04:58.199 --> 00:05:00.319
jump-cut solves so many pragmatic problems

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:01.600
it's not even funny

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.320
for the current iteration of the channel i use close-ups

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.480
to cover edits and to create emphasis

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.260
i am almost always on screen because

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:11.900
lip-reading is a fundamental pedagogical tool

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:15.180
many people can absorb information better or faster

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.080
when they are watching a face say it

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.340
rather than just hearing it and it minimizes my conflicts

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.460
with copyright robots by minimizing how much

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.600
copyright content i need to use on screen

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.200
however this in turn means that

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.660
everything needs to be recorded on camera

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.840
and because i have a personal desire to make

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.680
my image quality as good as i can

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.960
as a small way of standing out from the crowd

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.260
i don't want to make close-ups by just zooming in

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:42.180
like look at this, look at the drop in clarity

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.540
that's better

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.840
this all means that every video is recorded twice

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.060
at minimum all the way through

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.360
in order to get all the footage that's going to be needed

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:54.980
to weave everything together

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.720
which straight up doubles my record time

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.340
now that's me. that's my process

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.840
is it worth it?

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:02.660
maybe?

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.580
there is however no question

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.020
that using jump cuts instead

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.500
dramatically simplifies the process

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.900
and that's for me, in an environment where i have

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.020
the control, where i can record things multiple times

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.340
and i can change the camera configuration

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.820
the jump cut simply gets more valuable

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:21.860
the less control the creator has

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:23.520
or the more chaotic their environment

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.700
for people who use real environments again, that's real

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.320
with capital R as opposed to a poised environment

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:30.600
like a studio or set

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.820
there simply isn't another practical solution

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.580
for the vast majority of creators

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:35.660
the number of youtubers

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.140
running around with a small crew

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.180
who could even afford to have two cameras running

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.000
as they run around to make their stuff

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.800
is at most a few dozen

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.980
and even then it wouldn't necessarily serve

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.340
their aesthetic needs

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:47.480
at the end of the day

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.320
single camera confessional style is cheap and fast

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.240
and that's not a bad thing

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.600
because it still takes skill and thought

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.760
to do cheap and fast well

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.860
it's easier to use the space as a stage

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.480
or to use your environment as a canvas

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:06.260
than it is to fuss around with multiple camera setups

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.320
and all of the extra gear and hassle

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.460
that goes along with it

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.740
but it still takes forethought and creativity

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:13.760
to use those mechanisms well

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.760
to find a good canvas to know how and when

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.200
to use your stage to punctuate your points

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.320
but that skill and forethought is all something

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.060
that you can learn as someone running around

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.380
on their own with a camera

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.780
you don't need eight other people on your team

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.400
to figure out how to do it and how to do it well

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.960
so with all that i think that no matter

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.840
how many people post in the comments

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.120
about the youtube style and the jump cut

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.640
and how much they hate it

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.000
the jump cut is here to stay

